<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William, Count of Schaumburg-Lippe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1724">1724</a>–<a href="/years/1777">1777</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Wilhelm of Schaumburg-Lippe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William was count of Schaumburg-Lippe from 1748 to 1777 and a noted military reformer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. He commanded the Allied artillery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> and in 1762 reorganised the Portuguese army against a Spanish invasion. At home he built the island fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmstein">Wilhelmstein</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steinhuder-meer">Steinhuder Meer</a> and a military school there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-count-of-schaumburg-lippe.jpg" alt="William, Count of Schaumburg-Lippe" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Georg Ziesenis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wilhelm_(Schaumburg-Lippe).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Friedrich Wilhelm Ernst in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 9 January 1724, William was the son of Albrecht Wolfgang, Count of Schaumburg-Lippe, and his first wife, Countess Margarete Gertrud of Oeynhausen, who was said to be an illegitimate daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-i-of-great-britain">George I of Great Britain</a> and his mistress Ehrengard Melusine von der Schulenburg. He went with his father on campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> service in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>, was present at the Battle of Dettingen in 1743 and then fought in Austrian service in their Italian campaign. He succeeded his father as count on 25 October 1748.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As count and Generalfeldzeugmeister of the Electorate of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> he sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, led the allied artillery at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-minden">Battle of Minden</a> and was rewarded with command of all the allied artillery. He also held the rank of British field marshal. In 1762, at the request of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal">Marquis of Pombal</a>, he commanded the allied troops in Portugal against the Spanish invasion as generalissimo. Through marches and counter-marches he ensured that an enemy with a three-to-one superiority always met defenders in a good position and never risked an all-out attack. At Pombal's request he stayed for a year after the peace of 1763; two children of his, Joseph and Olimpia, were born at Elvas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 12 November 1765 he married Countess Marie Barbara Eleonore of Lippe-Biesterfeld; their daughter Emilie lived from 1771 to 1774, and a son born in 1772 died unnamed. He died at Wölpinghausen on 10 September 1777 and was succeeded by his cousin Philipp II Ernst of Lippe-Alverdissen. William was the first to work out a theory of purely defensive war, which he held to be the only kind justified on ethical grounds: &quot;Only defensive warfare is justified!&quot;</p><p>Its core was the fortified landscape, combining strongholds, an armed rural population and soldiers who worked the land in peacetime. His ideas, his support for universal conscription and his opposition to corporal punishment form a bridge to the people's war planned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Scharnhorst</a> and Gneisenau and to Scharnhorst's military reforms. A bust of him stands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walhalla">Walhalla</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William, Count of Schaumburg-Lippe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Count William</b>, in the 18th century, was a military reformer who built the island fortress of Wilhelmstein in the Steinhuder Meer and ran a school for officers where Gerhard von Scharnhorst trained.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-schaumburg-lippe">Principality of Schaumburg-Lippe</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Wölpinghausen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1758" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William, Count of Schaumburg-Lippe"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1758">What was here in 1758? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
